---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/Faq.astro";

import {
  lastVerified,
  correctionsUrl,
  tools,
  groups,
  rows,
  sources,
  status,
  UNDOCUMENTED,
} from "../../data/paas-compare.js";

const escapeHtml = (text) =>
  text.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;").replace(/"/g, "&quot;");

const renderInline = (text) =>
  escapeHtml(text).replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code class="rounded bg-white/[0.06] px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[12px] text-fg">$1</code>');

const sourceLabel = (url) => url.replace(/^https:\/\//, "").replace(/\/$/, "");

const sourceLinks = (codes) =>
  codes
    .flatMap((code) => sources[code])
    .map((url) => `<a href="${url}" class="text-fg-faint underline decoration-fg-faint/50 underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">${sourceLabel(url)}</a>`)
    .join(", ");

const plain = (text) => text.replace(/`/g, "");

const cellLabel = (c) =>
  c.s === "value"
    ? c.full
    : c.short
      ? c.s === UNDOCUMENTED
        ? `${status[c.s].word} (${c.short})`
        : `${status[c.s].word}: ${c.short}`
      : status[c.s].word;

const noteHtml = (c) => {
  const head =
    c.s === "value"
      ? renderInline(c.full)
      : c.s === UNDOCUMENTED
        ? c.short
          ? `Not documented (${renderInline(c.short)})`
          : "Not documented"
        : `${status[c.s].icon} ${renderInline(c.short || status[c.s].word)}`;
  const note = c.note ? ` ${renderInline(c.note)}` : "";
  const links = c.src.length ? ` <span class="whitespace-normal">${sourceLinks(c.src)}</span>` : "";
  return `${head}.${note}${links}`;
};

const tableGroups = groups.map((name) => ({ name, rows: rows.filter((row) => row.group === name) }));
const noteGroups = [...tableGroups, { name: "Not in the table", rows: rows.filter((row) => !row.group) }];

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Is Coolify or Dokploy better?",
    answer:
      "It depends on what you need. Both are open-source dashboards with Compose support, backups, previews, an API, a CLI and a managed cloud. Coolify offers more one-click services, previews on four Git providers and a built-in MCP server. Dokploy has Docker Swarm clustering built in and per-member permissions in its free edition.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which of these runs on the smallest server?",
    answer:
      "Kamal adds no control plane, because it runs from your machine. Of the server-side tools, CapRover and Dokku document 1 GB RAM. Coolify and Dokploy document 2 GB.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Coolify support GitLab, Bitbucket and Gitea, and volume backups?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Coolify integrates with GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket and Gitea for deploys and preview deployments. Scheduled volume and directory-mount backups (local or S3) were added in v4.3.0.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which tools have a managed cloud version?",
    answer:
      "Coolify Cloud and Dokploy Cloud both host the dashboard while your apps run on your own servers. CapRover Pro and Dokku Pro are paid add-ons, not hosting, and Kamal has no paid offering.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which ones support Docker Compose?",
    answer:
      "Coolify and Dokploy deploy Compose files natively. CapRover supports an experimental subset. Dokku and Kamal don't deploy Compose files.",
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "Coolify vs Dokploy, CapRover, Dokku and Kamal",
  dateModified: lastVerified,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "coolLabs", url: "https://coollabs.io" },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "coolLabs", url: "https://coollabs.io" },
  mainEntityOfPage: "https://coolify.io/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal",
};

const th = "px-4 py-3 text-xs font-medium whitespace-nowrap text-fg";
const td = "px-4 py-2.5 align-middle text-[13px] leading-5 text-fg-dim";
---

<Layout
  title="Coolify vs Dokploy, CapRover, Dokku and Kamal"
  canonicalUrl="https://coolify.io/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal"
  description="Coolify vs Dokploy, plus CapRover, Dokku and Kamal. Sourced comparison of features, server size, licenses and cloud options, and when each is the better pick."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <section class="px-4">
    <p class="eyebrow">Compare</p>
    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-fg md:text-5xl">
      Coolify vs Dokploy, CapRover, Dokku and Kamal<span class="text-warning">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-2xl text-sm text-pretty text-fg-faint italic">
      Last verified: <time datetime={lastVerified}>{lastVerified}</time>. Written by the Coolify team, so we have a stake
      in this. We've tried to be fair, the notes and sources for every cell are in the
      <a href="#notes" class="underline decoration-fg-faint underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">"Notes and sources"</a>
      section below the table, linking to each tool's own documentation, and we list where the others are better. Spot something wrong or out of date? Please tell us (see
      <a href="#corrections" class="underline decoration-fg-faint underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">"Corrections"</a>
      below).
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <Prose>
      <p class="text-base md:text-lg">
        <strong>Summary:</strong> Coolify, Dokploy and CapRover are self-hosted PaaS dashboards; Dokku is a CLI-first,
        Heroku-style PaaS, and Kamal deploys from your own machine with no control plane on the server. Coolify has the
        broadest built-in feature set, Dokploy is stronger for Docker Swarm clustering and per-member permissions, and
        CapRover and Dokku run on less RAM.
      </p>

      <h2>Head-to-head pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy">Coolify vs Dokploy</a> covers the two dashboard tools on their own:
          Swarm, permissions, Compose and cloud pricing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/vercel-alternative">Coolify as a Vercel alternative</a>: a server you administer, compared
          with Vercel's hosted platform.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/heroku-alternative">Coolify as a Heroku alternative</a>: a dashboard on your VPS, compared
          with Heroku dynos and managed data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/compare/netlify-alternative">Coolify as a Netlify alternative</a>: Docker apps on your server,
          compared with Netlify's CDN, functions and managed Postgres.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Which should you pick?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You want one dashboard for apps, databases and ready-made services on one or a few servers → Coolify.</strong>
          It also fits if you want backups, previews and team access built in, or would rather not host the dashboard
          yourself (<a href="/pricing">Coolify Cloud</a>). New to this? See
          <a href="/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps">how to deploy apps on your own VPS</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You want Docker Swarm clustering from a UI, or fine-grained permissions per member → Dokploy.</strong>
          Its free edition lets you grant Members specific permissions. Custom roles are in Dokploy Enterprise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You have a 1 GB server and want a simple UI with a single Swarm-based model → CapRover.</strong> It
          documents a 1 GB minimum, deploys straight from your machine with <code>caprover deploy</code>, and lets you
          edit the full NGINX config per app.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You like Heroku's <code>git push</code> workflow and are happy in a terminal → Dokku.</strong> It runs
          on a single host by default and needs 1 GB. An optional k3s scheduler handles multiple nodes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You want to deploy a containerized app from your laptop or CI to one or many servers, with nothing
          extra running on them → Kamal.</strong> It uses zero-downtime deploys behind kamal-proxy, and was built for
          Rails but works with any containerized web app.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="feature-comparison">Feature comparison</h2>
      <p class="text-sm">
        ✅ built in · ◐ partial or paid add-on · ❌ not available. Details and sources are in the notes below the table.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </article>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-4xl px-4 text-left" aria-labelledby="feature-comparison">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg ring-1 ring-hairline">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[44rem] table-fixed border-collapse">
        <colgroup>
          <col class="w-56" />
          {tools.map(() => <col />)}
        </colgroup>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-hairline bg-white/[0.03]">
            <th scope="col" class={`${th} sticky left-0 z-10 bg-app text-left`}>Feature</th>
            {
              tools.map((tool) => (
                <th scope="col" class={`${th} text-center`}>
                  <a href={tool.url} class="hover:text-warning transition-colors">{tool.name}</a>
                </th>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </thead>
        {
          tableGroups.map((group) => (
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline border-b border-hairline last:border-b-0">
              <tr>
                <th scope="colgroup" colspan={tools.length + 1} class="sticky left-0 bg-app px-4 pt-5 pb-2 text-left">
                  <span class="eyebrow">{group.name}</span>
                </th>
              </tr>
              {group.rows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class={`${td} sticky left-0 z-10 bg-app text-left font-medium text-fg`}>
                    {row.feature}
                    {row.hint && <span class="block text-xs font-normal text-fg-faint">{row.hint}</span>}
                  </th>
                  {row.cells.map((c) => (
                    <td class={`${td} text-center`}>
                      {c.s === "value" ? (
                        <span title={c.full !== c.value ? plain(c.full) : undefined}>{c.value}</span>
                      ) : c.s === UNDOCUMENTED ? (
                        <span title={c.short ? plain(cellLabel(c)) : undefined} class="text-xs text-fg-faint">
                          Not documented
                        </span>
                      ) : (
                        <span
                          role="img"
                          aria-label={plain(cellLabel(c))}
                          title={c.short ? plain(cellLabel(c)) : undefined}
                          class={c.short ? "cursor-help" : undefined}
                        >
                          {status[c.s].icon}
                        </span>
                      )}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          ))
        }
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <details id="notes" class="group card">
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-fg [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
        Notes and sources
        <span class="text-xs font-normal text-fg-faint group-open:hidden">Show per-row details</span>
        <span class="hidden text-xs font-normal text-fg-faint group-open:inline">Hide</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="space-y-8 border-t border-hairline px-4 py-5">
        {
          noteGroups.map((group) => (
            <section>
              <h3 class="eyebrow">{group.name}</h3>
              <div class="mt-3 space-y-5">
                {group.rows.map((row) => (
                  <div>
                    <h4 class="text-sm font-medium text-fg">
                      {row.feature}
                      {row.hint && <span class="font-normal text-fg-faint"> ({row.hint})</span>}
                    </h4>
                    <ul class="mt-1.5 space-y-1 text-[13px] leading-5 text-fg-dim">
                      {row.cells.map((c, i) => (
                        <li class="wrap-anywhere">
                          <span class="font-medium text-fg">{tools[i].name}:</span> <Fragment set:html={noteHtml(c)} />
                        </li>
                      ))}
                    </ul>
                    {row.note && <p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] leading-5 text-fg-dim" set:html={renderInline(row.note)} />}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </section>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </details>

    <Prose>
      <h2>Where the others are stronger</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Clustering:</strong> Dokploy and CapRover have Docker Swarm built in. Coolify's multi-server deploys
          need your own load balancer and don't yet cover Compose apps or apps with persistent storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Permissions:</strong> Dokploy lets you grant Members specific permissions in its free edition.
          Coolify's roles are team-wide.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Footprint:</strong> CapRover and Dokku document 1 GB minimums, and Kamal adds no control plane to your
          servers. Coolify documents 2 GB.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Workflow fit:</strong> Dokku is the closest to Heroku's <code>git push</code>. Kamal is the most direct
          path from a repo to servers if you don't want a dashboard. CapRover lets you edit the full NGINX template per
          app.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Coolify</h2>
      <p>
        An open-source (Apache-2.0), self-hostable alternative to Vercel, Heroku, Netlify and Railway. You get a web
        dashboard for apps, databases and <a href="/services">360+ one-click services</a> on any server you can reach
        over SSH. It has built-in database and volume backups to S3, preview deployments for GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket
        and Gitea, team roles, an audit log, an API, a CLI and an MCP server. You self-host it for free with every
        feature, or use Coolify Cloud ($5/month for 2 servers + $3 per extra). Trade-offs: it needs
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/start-with-self-hosted">2 GB RAM</a>, Swarm support is deprecated, and roles
        are team-wide only.
      </p>

      <h2>Dokploy</h2>
      <p>
        See also the shorter <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy">Coolify vs Dokploy</a> page. Dokploy is
        a self-hostable PaaS with a web UI, Docker Compose support, Docker Swarm clustering, Traefik, database and volume
        backups, previews (GitHub), scheduled jobs, monitoring and notifications. Its free edition has per-member
        permissions, and Enterprise adds SSO, SCIM, custom roles and audit logs. There's a managed Dokploy Cloud from
        $4.50/month per server. Most of the code is Apache-2.0, and a <code>/proprietary</code> folder is
        source-available. It needs 2 GB RAM and 30 GB disk.
        (<a href="https://docs.dokploy.com/docs/core/installation">Dokploy docs</a>)
      </p>

      <h2>CapRover</h2>
      <p>
        A free, Apache-2.0 PaaS built on Docker, NGINX, Let's Encrypt and Docker Swarm, with a dashboard, a CLI
        (<code>caprover deploy</code>), one-click apps, one-click rollbacks and NetData monitoring. Compose support is an
        experimental subset, and backups don't cover volumes. A paid Pro plan adds build notifications, login alerts and
        2FA. It documents the lowest RAM minimum of the dashboard tools here (1 GB).
        (<a href="https://caprover.com/docs/get-started">CapRover docs</a>)
      </p>

      <h2>Dokku</h2>
      <p>
        An MIT-licensed, Heroku-style PaaS: <code>git push</code> to your server, builds with buildpacks or Dockerfiles,
        and features added through plugins (databases, Let's Encrypt). It's CLI-first. The web UI, REST API and team
        management are in Dokku Pro, a paid self-hosted add-on. It runs on one host by default (1 GB RAM), and an
        optional k3s scheduler spans several nodes.
        (<a href="https://dokku.com/docs/getting-started/installation/">Dokku docs</a>)
      </p>

      <h2>Kamal</h2>
      <p>
        An MIT-licensed deploy tool from 37signals. You run it from your machine or CI. It connects to servers over SSH,
        installs Docker if missing, and does zero-downtime deploys behind kamal-proxy. There's no dashboard and no
        server-side control plane. Databases are "accessories" you configure, and multi-server setups need your own load
        balancer. It was built for Rails but works with any containerized web app.
        (<a href="https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/installation/">Kamal docs</a>)
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Tools change quickly. If a cell here is wrong or out of date, especially for Dokploy, CapRover, Dokku or Kamal,
        please tell us at <a href={correctionsUrl}><strong>github.com/coollabsio/coolify.io/issues</strong></a> with a
        link to the official docs, and we'll update the page and the "last verified" date.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </article>
</Layout>
